1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,420
You open a jar of pickles.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:02,680
It doesn’t resist.

3
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:04,160
It doesn’t even try.

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,560
The lid just...

5
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,980
gives, like it was never closed.

6
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:10,340
You help your neighbor move a couch.

7
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:11,540
One hand.

8
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:12,520
She stares.

9
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:13,720
How...

10
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,240
how did you do that?

11
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:16,180
You shrug.

12
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:17,540
Protein shake...

13
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:18,280
you say.

14
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:19,980
She doesn’t believe you.

15
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:23,180
Later you squeeze a tennis ball just to see.

16
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:24,080
It pops.

17
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,020
Did you mean to do that?

18
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,500
Your little brother laughs so hard he falls off the chair.

19
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,120
Dinner’s normal though.

20
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:31,960
Spaghetti.

21
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:32,920
Garlic bread.

22
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:34,800
Your fork still works fine.

23
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:38,580
An average adult’s grip strength is about four hundred newtons.

24
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:42,840
Enough to open most jars, most doors, most ordinary mornings.

25
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:43,980
And you?

26
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:46,400
Closer to four thousand.

27
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,120
Ten times the force, same two hands.

28
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:52,760
But here’s the rule, the one that runs this whole story.

29
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:54,820
Your muscles got stronger.

30
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:57,180
Your tendons didn’t.

31
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:58,920
Your bones didn’t.

32
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,460
The ropes and rods holding you together are still...

33
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:03,040
exactly what they were.

34
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,180
So what happens when the strongest part of you starts

35
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:07,180
pulling on the weakest?

36
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:11,080
At school, you carry three friends’ backpacks without blinking.

37
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:13,280
Plus: no more sore shoulders.

38
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,560
Minus: you grip a locker handle too hard...

39
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:18,560
and it just comes off in your hand.

40
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:19,980
Everyone stares.

41
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:21,100
Seriously?

42
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
You mumble something about cheap metal.

43
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,320
On the basketball court, you don’t dunk so much as

44
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:27,740
you launch.

45
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:29,320
The rim bends.

46
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,300
Coach blows the whistle twice.

47
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:34,140
No jumping off two feet, he says.

48
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:34,840
Got it?

49
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,120
That’s a new rule now.

50
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:38,020
Track and field is worse.

51
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,270
The world record for a standing long jump is three

52
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:43,800
point seven one meters, set in 2015.

53
00:01:44,530 --> 00:01:47,000
You clear five meters on your first try...

54
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,220
and land wrong.

55
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:49,800
Your ankle screams.

56
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:51,200
Not broken.

57
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:52,180
Not yet.

58
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:53,380
Just a warning.

59
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:56,320
Your friend Theo dares you to arm wrestle him.

60
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:58,340
You win in half a second.

61
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:00,000
You’re kidding me, right?

62
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:01,440
He laughs it off...

63
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:03,520
but there’s something else in his eyes.

64
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:05,200
Something careful.

65
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,640
At home, you try to help your mom carry in groceries.

66
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:10,420
Four bags at once.

67
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:11,680
The paper tears.

68
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:13,280
Cans roll everywhere.

69
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:14,640
She just...

70
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:15,780
watches you.

71
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:16,520
Quietly.

72
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:18,980
Then the scout shows up.

73
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:20,360
A man named Mr.

74
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:22,580
Hale, from some private sports academy.

75
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:25,340
He films you lifting the back end of a car

76
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:26,720
to retrieve a dropped phone.

77
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:28,880
Incredible, he says.

78
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:30,240
We could make you famous.

79
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:31,800
We could make you money.

80
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:32,920
Big money.

81
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:34,440
Who wouldn’t want that?

82
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:36,400
He doesn’t ask if you’re okay.

83
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:38,520
He never once asks that.

84
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:43,000
Usain Bolt’s fastest recorded speed was about forty-four point seven

85
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,220
kilometers per hour, back in 2009.

86
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:46,680
You’re not that fast.

87
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:48,060
Your legs are normal.

88
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:51,480
Your muscles react in a normal brain’s normal time.

89
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:54,800
The strength is only in the pull, not the speed.

90
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:56,500
So you’re powerful...

91
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:57,940
but not quick.

92
00:02:59,000 --> 00:02:59,920
Powerful and clumsy.

93
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:02,660
A strange, dangerous combination.

94
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:05,740
And there’s a cost nobody warns you about.

95
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:06,860
The hunger.

96
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:10,480
Ten times the force means ten times the fuel,

97
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:12,100
or close to it.

98
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:13,800
You’re eating constantly.

99
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:15,400
Second breakfast.

100
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:16,560
Third breakfast.

101
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:17,640
Is that...

102
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:18,960
a fourth sandwich?

103
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:20,200
Your mom asks.

104
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:21,480
Growth spurt...

105
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:22,500
you mutter.

106
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:27,540
Headaches too, some nights, like your whole body is still recalibrating.

107
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:28,740
And the fear.

108
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:30,160
The real one.

109
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:33,880
Every handshake, every hug, every high five...

110
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:35,600
you have to think first.

111
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:36,840
Calculate.

112
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:37,700
Hold back.

113
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:40,760
Normal kids don’t think before they hug someone.

114
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:41,900
You do.

115
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:43,800
Every single time.

116
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,080
Your mom notices you flinching before touching the dog.

117
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:48,800
Hey, she says.

118
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:49,760
What’s wrong?

119
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:50,920
Talk to me.

120
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:54,400
You tell her about the locker, the jar, the ankle.

121
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:56,200
She doesn’t scold you.

122
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:57,980
She just holds up her hand...

123
00:03:58,600 --> 00:03:58,780
slowly.

124
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:00,000
Palm out.

125
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:01,800
Gentle, she says.

126
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:02,800
Like this.

127
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:04,460
It becomes your thing.

128
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:06,240
A gentle high five.

129
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:08,020
Soft as a secret.

130
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:10,240
It means I see you.

131
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:11,920
It means be careful.

132
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:15,440
It means I love you, whatever you can lift.

133
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:17,260
You don’t say anything back.

134
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:18,620
You don’t need to.

135
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:19,500
Mr.

136
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:20,460
Hale calls again.

137
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:21,580
There’s an event.

138
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:25,680
A charity strongman show, live, cameras everywhere.

139
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:28,140
Pull a loaded truck down Main Street.

140
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:30,660
Easy money for the foundation, he says.

141
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:32,340
Great exposure for you.

142
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:33,260
You hesitate.

143
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:35,140
Then Theo texts.

144
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,940
Kid stuck under a collapsed scaffold two blocks from the venue.

145
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:39,760
Not staged.

146
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:40,580
Real.

147
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:41,700
You run.

148
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:43,020
The truck is forgotten.

149
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,680
The scaffold beam is maybe two hundred kilograms of steel,

150
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:47,760
pinning a leg.

151
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:49,300
You could lift it...

152
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,360
but lifting fast means snapping it up in an instant,

153
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,340
and a sudden jolt like that could crush what’s underneath

154
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:56,180
even worse.

155
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:57,480
Lift it wrong...

156
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,240
and the leg goes with it.

157
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:00,820
Slow is safer.

158
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:02,940
Slow is agony.

159
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:05,740
Every second the kid cries out.

160
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:06,560
Mr.

161
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:09,220
Hale is screaming about the show starting in ten minutes.

162
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:10,280
Did you hear me?

163
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:11,260
Ten minutes...

164
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:12,620
or this boy’s leg.

165
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:13,580
You choose.

166
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:16,360
You lift the beam the way your mom taught you

167
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:16,980
to high five.

168
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:17,760
Gentle.

169
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:19,360
Painfully slow.

170
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:22,120
Your arms shake, not from weakness...

171
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:26,560
from control, from holding ten times the force back on

172
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:26,900
a leash.

173
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:29,040
Theo slides the kid free.

174
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:33,580
The crowd that gathered for a truck-pull instead watches something real.

175
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:34,460
Mr.

176
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:36,660
Hale is furious about the missed show.

177
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:37,940
What were you thinking?

178
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:39,320
You don’t care.

179
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,220
You find your mom in the crowd.

180
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:43,700
She raises her hand, palm out.

181
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:44,860
You match it.

182
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:49,300
Gentle, she whispers, and this time it means something different.

183
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:50,760
It means proud.

184
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:53,340
Is any of this actually possible?

185
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:54,720
Partly.

186
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:58,640
Human tendons really can take forces several times greater than

187
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:01,800
body weight during a hard jump, which is exactly why

188
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:04,840
injuries like torn patellar tendons happen to real athletes.

189
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:09,048
The current deadlift world record is five hundred and one

190
00:06:09,048 --> 00:06:11,780
kilograms, set by Hafþór Björnsson in 2020,

191
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:15,480
proof that human muscle has serious untapped ceilings.

192
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:16,620
But...

193
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:20,320
multiplying raw strength by ten, all at once,

194
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:22,540
with no upgrade to bone or tendon?

195
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:24,260
That part is fiction.

196
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:27,260
Your skeleton is the real limiting factor,

197
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:29,040
long before your muscles are.

198
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:30,000
So...

199
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,900
could strength ever be safely separated from the structure holding

200
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:34,280
it up?

201
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:36,680
That night, your phone buzzes.

202
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:38,040
Unknown number.

203
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:39,200
One word.

204
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:40,400
Impressive...

205
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:42,540
Then nothing else.

206
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,660
Who sends a text like that?

207
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:46,560
So, what do you think?

208
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:49,760
Tell us in the comments, and suggest the next superpower

209
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:50,800
you want to see.

210
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:53,300
And if that gentle high five got you...

211
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:56,140
like and subscribe for more What If episodes.
